{
  "ruling": {
    "ruling": "ESCALATE",
    "amount_sgd": null,
    "driver_compensation_sgd": null,
    "referrals": [
      "POL-NS-25: rider_profile.no_show_cancellations_30d stands at 3, at or above the clause's threshold of 3 (counting this trip if its fee stands would make it 4), so the rider's account is flagged for review — mandatory upfront payment and temporary suspension pending Trust & Safety review. This referral never changes the ruling on the trip."
    ],
    "confidence": 0.85,
    "explanation": "To the rider: your SGD 5.00 no-show charge for trip TRP-05059 is not refunded today, and it is not upheld today either — the case goes to a human reviewer and the SGD 5.00 stays held rather than collected or refunded. Two of your points are settled against you on the record. On 'cancelled too fast': the driver arrived at Block 401, Ang Mo Kio Avenue 10 at 08:29:10, and the driver-device trace (EV-GPS-05059, COMPLETE) corroborates that arrival — its nearest fix is 6.5 m from the pickup, inside the 50 m STANDARD arrival radius, and the trace is stationary there from 08:29 — so POL-NS-21's free wait of 300 s ran out at 08:34:10 and cancellation came at 08:35:30, 380 s after arrival, some 80 s past expiry. On 'did not even see his message': POL-NS-23 requires the driver to send at least one in-app message and place at least one in-app call before cancelling; it does not require you to read or answer. Two messages (08:29:30 and 08:32:45) and one call (08:33:40, ringing 25 s unanswered) are on the record before the 08:35:30 cancellation. The fare ledger (EV-FARE-05059, COMPLETE) carries one cancellation-fee line of SGD 5.00 charged at 08:35:34 with refund_issued_sgd 0.00, so there was no second charge. To the driver: POL-NS-21 and POL-NS-23 are met and the SGD 5.00 is exactly the fee POL-NS-22 fixes, but the fee cannot be confirmed on this record. POL-NS-24 refunds the fee where the driver failed to arrive by the scheduled pickup time plus 5 minutes, and its own note says that where an on-demand trip's record carries no scheduled time and the deadline cannot be fixed, POL-GEN-26 applies. This trip is on-demand: scheduled_at is null and the record's driver-deadline block returns deadline null for the reason 'on-demand trip: no scheduled time'. The late-arrival question POL-NS-24 raises therefore cannot be answered from this record, and POL-GEN-26 requires the case to go to a human reviewer with that open question stated, rather than an outcome being inferred from the gap; your own claim that the deadline 'cannot be fixed' concedes the fact that puts the case there. On the claims: the fee-stands claims (free wait served on the 380 s dwell, arrival corroborated by the trace, contact made, SGD 5.00 equal to policy_fee_sgd, and the AI-finality argument under POL-GEN-22/POL-NS-26) are accepted on the merits, but none of them resolves POL-NS-24, and POL-NS-26 itself makes no-show finality subject to POL-GEN-26, so they do not carry a DRIVER_COMPENSATION ruling today. The rider's claim that the empty rider side of the trace (rider_track empty) leaves the no-show unproven is rejected: no clause of this version requires rider-side location evidence — the no-show test is the driver's wait under POL-NS-21, read off the trip record and the trace — and the escalation here rests on POL-NS-24/POL-GEN-26, not on that. The rider's point that the contact facts sit on the impaired chat log EV-CHAT-05059 (weight 0.85, issue UNREAD_MESSAGE) is accepted, and is why the confidence stated here is 0.85. The rider's suggestion that escalation is simply the outcome that spares the rider is not itself a ground — escalation follows only from POL-GEN-26, which is what produces it here. Both parties' profile history was correctly kept out of the outcome under POL-GEN-01, except rider_profile.no_show_cancellations_30d, which POL-NS-25 names for the referral above. The SGD 5.00 remains held pending review."
  },
  "discarded_claims": []
}
